# The cases a Bridge's harness executes for this adapter, and how each is
# judged, as a test manifest in the shape every W3C RDF test suite uses:
# W3C's mf: test-manifest vocabulary, and on top of it the test terms of the
# Cascade Bridge vocabulary bridge:, declared in ../schema/manifest/bridge.ttl
# and constrained by ../schema/manifest/bridge.shapes.ttl (SHACL), the way
# the SHACL test suite adds sht:. Each entry has a type, an mf:action (the
# input) and an mf:result (the expected output); the comparison rule is
# carried by the entry's type. Nothing here executes in this repository:
# version 1 of the harness runs in bridge-engine-java, version 2 in
# bridge-engine-browser.
#
# Relative IRIs resolve against this file's location, so <in/VCV000017661-BRCA1.xml>
# names the same file the crate (../ro-crate-metadata.json, the adapter's
# manifest) names as fixtures/in/VCV000017661-BRCA1.xml, a dataset and an
# envelope are named by the crate's own @id, <../> is the crate's root
# entity (the adapter, whose bridge:testManifest is this file), and <> is
# this file, the crate's fixtures/manifest.ttl entity. Loaded together, the
# manifest and the crate are one graph: from an entry to its input's digest
# and licence, or from its envelope to the schema that validates it, is a
# walk, not a string match. Where every file and dataset named here came
# from is in the crate.
#
# A comparison is insensitive to everything the format does not mean. That is
# the one principle the rules below apply twice: an RDF graph does not mean
# its blank node labels, so they are relabelled before comparing; a JSON array
# of findings does not mean its element order, so it is compared as a
# multiset. A stricter rule than the format's meaning does not catch more
# mapping errors, it only fails harnesses that are right.
#
# The comparison rule, stated once (RFC section 16 says it comes first):
#   bridge:IsomorphicConversionTest
#                      graph isomorphic: blank nodes relabelled, IRIs and
#                      literals exact. The existing oracle in
#                      cascade-cli/tests/clinvar-conformance.test.ts compares
#                      `riot --output=nq | sort` byte for byte, which assumes
#                      the same thing; RDFC-1.0 decides it properly.
#                      findings as a multiset: the array a Bridge produces
#                      must hold exactly the entries the expected sidecar
#                      holds, with the same multiplicity and no others. Order
#                      is not compared. Two entries are equal when they have
#                      the same member names and equal values (JSON object
#                      equality), not when their bytes match. Multiplicity is
#                      compared: entries do repeat, the repeats are identical,
#                      and a set comparison would silently lose them.
#                        Order is left out deliberately, and this is a change
#                        from the rule this file first carried. The committed
#                        sidecars are in the order cascade-cli's authoring
#                        script left them: localeCompare on (sourceField,
#                        severity, reason), which is ICU collation, in which
#                        '/' sorts before '@' even though U+002F is above
#                        U+0040. A harness comparing with String.compareTo in
#                        Java, or with < in JavaScript, puts the same
#                        correctly-mapped entries in a different order and
#                        would report a false failure on every entry here.
#                        Requiring the committed order instead would make
#                        every Bridge carry ICU to compare a bookkeeping
#                        array, and would pin a CLDR version nothing states.
#                        Nothing in the findings model refers to a position,
#                        so the order carries no meaning and is not part of
#                        the contract.
#                        Separately, and as file hygiene rather than
#                        judgement: a sidecar written from scratch SHOULD be
#                        sorted by Unicode code point on (sourceField,
#                        severity, reason), because every language reproduces
#                        code point order identically and it keeps
#                        regeneration diffs readable. This is a property of
#                        the file, never of the comparison. The four sidecars
#                        here do not follow it and are not re-sorted: they are
#                        verbatim copies whose digests the crate records and
#                        whose blobs must stay identical to conformance at
#                        0ea48bb. Under the rule above their order is simply
#                        incidental, which is the point.
#   bridge:ignorePredicate
#                      the stamp triples a Bridge adds after the mapping (RFC
#                      section 5, "stamp"; EIP Message History), removed from
#                      both sides first. The stamp set is a property of the
#                      Bridge's stamp stage, not of any one fixture, so it is
#                      stated once, on the manifest, and every entry inherits
#                      it; an entry that carries its own bridge:ignorePredicate
#                      replaces the manifest's set for that entry alone.
#                      cascade:dataProvenance and cascade:schemaVersion are
#                      what cascade-cli's ClinVar converter writes on every IRI
#                      subject (inline, in simple-allele.ts,
#                      rcv-interpretation.ts and scv-submitter-assertion.ts; it
#                      has no shared helper) and what the oracles carry.
#                      Import time is the third thing the RFC's stamp stage
#                      names; no oracle carries one and the cli emits none, so
#                      prov:generatedAtTime is a placeholder for the Bridge to
#                      confirm or replace.
#   bridge:InputOnlyTest
#                      parse and validate the input; the graph and findings a
#                      Bridge produces are recorded, not judged.
#   bridge:DatasetCompletionTest
#                      stream the dataset in its envelope; the run must
#                      complete without rejection; the record count and output
#                      digest must match where present and are recorded where
#                      absent.

@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix mf:      <http://www.w3.org/2001/sw/DataAccess/tests/test-manifest#> .
@prefix bridge:  <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/bridge/v1-draft#> .
@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

<> a mf:Manifest ;
  rdfs:label "cascade-bridge-adapter-clinvar fixture cases" ;
  bridge:adapter <../> ; # the crate's root entity, the adapter; its bridge:testManifest is <>
  bridge:ignorePredicate cascade:dataProvenance, cascade:schemaVersion, prov:generatedAtTime ; # the stamp set every entry inherits; see the header
  mf:entries (
    <#VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508>
    <#VCV000017661-BRCA1>
    <#VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic>
    <#VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome>
    <#VCV_XML_VCV000091629>
    <#clinvar-vcv-release-2026-09>
  ) .

# The four conformance oracles. File names are the conformance repository's
# and are kept so each copy stays traceable; two of them do not describe the
# record inside (noted per entry). Content is byte-identical to conformance.
# None carries its own bridge:ignorePredicate: the manifest's stamp set
# applies to all four. Every entry arrives in the efetch envelope, the crate's
# #envelope-efetch.

<#VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508> a bridge:IsomorphicConversionTest ;
  mf:name "VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508" ;
  rdfs:comment "NM_000492.3(CFTR):c.1521_1523del (p.Phe508del), version 206, last updated 2026-05-03. The largest oracle: 99 submissions." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:input <in/VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508.xml> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-efetch>
  ] ;
  mf:result [
    bridge:graph <expected/VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508.ttl> ;
    bridge:findings <findings/VCV000007105-CFTR-deltaF508.gaps.json>
  ] .

<#VCV000017661-BRCA1> a bridge:IsomorphicConversionTest ;
  mf:name "VCV000017661-BRCA1" ;
  rdfs:comment "NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.181T>G (p.Cys61Gly), version 157, last updated 2026-05-03. The RFC's worked example (about eighty IRI subjects)." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:input <in/VCV000017661-BRCA1.xml> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-efetch>
  ] ;
  mf:result [
    bridge:graph <expected/VCV000017661-BRCA1.ttl> ;
    bridge:findings <findings/VCV000017661-BRCA1.gaps.json>
  ] .

<#VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic> a bridge:IsomorphicConversionTest ;
  mf:name "VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic" ;
  rdfs:comment "NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.5193+1G>C, version 15, last updated 2026-04-13. The file name says BRCA2; the record is a BRCA1 variant." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:input <in/VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic.xml> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-efetch>
  ] ;
  mf:result [
    bridge:graph <expected/VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic.ttl> ;
    bridge:findings <findings/VCV000055448-BRCA2-pathogenic.gaps.json>
  ] .

<#VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome> a bridge:IsomorphicConversionTest ;
  mf:name "VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome" ;
  rdfs:comment "NM_001017980.4(VMA21):c.164-6T>G, version 2, last updated 2025-08-23, one submission. The file name says MLH1 and Lynch syndrome; the record is a VMA21 variant interpreted for MONDO:0010684. The smallest oracle." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:input <in/VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome.xml> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-efetch>
  ] ;
  mf:result [
    bridge:graph <expected/VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome.ttl> ;
    bridge:findings <findings/VCV000208804-MLH1-LynchSyndrome.gaps.json>
  ] .

# NCBI's official efetch sample: the one input whose provenance is
# unimpeachable. No expected output has been produced for it, so the entry
# carries no mf:result.

<#VCV_XML_VCV000091629> a bridge:InputOnlyTest ;
  mf:name "VCV_XML_VCV000091629" ;
  rdfs:comment "NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4357+2T>G, version 19, last updated 2026-02-15, from pub/clinvar/xml/sample_xml/. Parsed and validated; the graph a Bridge produces is recorded, not judged." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:input <in/VCV_XML_VCV000091629.xml> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-efetch>
  ] .

# Referenced datasets, too large to commit, in the release envelope, the
# crate's #envelope-release. A multi-gigabyte expected output fits in one
# line: the record count and a digest of the canonical output. RDF has no
# null, so both are absent until the first run in bridge-engine-java records
# them, as
#   mf:result [ bridge:records <n> ; bridge:outputDigest "sha256:<hex>" ]
# where bridge:records is the number of VariationArchive units processed and
# bridge:outputDigest the sha256 of the canonical N-Quads output, stamp
# triples removed. A dataset test names a crate Dataset a Bridge can fetch,
# one with a contentUrl (the shapes check this), so the weekly release is not
# listed: on 2026-09-06 its symlink resolved to the monthly file below, and an
# entry for it would be a second pass over the same bytes. It is added, as a
# dated file with its own crate entity, when one exists (docs/format.md,
# "Releases").

<#clinvar-vcv-release-2026-09> a bridge:DatasetCompletionTest ;
  mf:name "clinvar-vcv-release-2026-09" ;
  rdfs:comment "The monthly VCV release of 2026-09 (ReleaseDate 2026-08-29), about 5.8 GB gzipped, in the ClinVarVariationRelease envelope." ;
  mf:action [
    bridge:dataset <https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pub/clinvar/xml/ClinVarVCVRelease_2026-09.xml.gz> ;
    bridge:envelope <../ro-crate-metadata.json#envelope-release>
  ] .
  # mf:result absent until the first run records bridge:records and bridge:outputDigest.
